---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy Policy · Pangaea"
  description="How Pangaea handles PostHog analytics, fonts, embeds, cookies, local writing data, feeds, the Substack newsletter, and links to external services."
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>

  <p class="meta">Last updated: 10 November 2026.</p>

  <p>
    Pangaea is Adam Pang's personal publication. This policy describes the data behavior of
    pangaea.blog as it is currently built, including the public reading pages and the authoring
    tools on the same domain. Questions about this policy use the <a href="/contact/">Pangaea
    contact path</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Public reading</h2>
  <p>
    Public pages do not provide reader accounts, comments, an on-site newsletter signup form, an
    on-site contact form, advertising pixels, or a paid subscription. The initial public response
    does not set a Pangaea cookie. Vercel hosts the site and processes ordinary request information
    needed to serve pages, protect the service, and operate its infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h2>Email newsletter</h2>
  <p>
    The Email and newsletter links lead to Adam Pang's newsletter on Substack
    (adampang.substack.com). Pangaea does not collect email addresses itself. Anyone who subscribes
    does so on Substack, and Substack's own terms and privacy policy apply to that signup.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    Pangaea uses PostHog (US cloud, us.i.posthog.com) for product analytics. The script records
    page views and page leaves, with the page URL, referrer, and the site hostname attached.
    General click autocapture, session recording, and surveys are switched off in the site code,
    and the site does not identify readers or create person profiles. The PostHog project settings
    also enable two optional measurements: page performance timings (Core Web Vitals) and dead-click
    events, which note a click that produced no visible change on the page. PostHog receives the request IP address and
    browser details that come with each event, and its script stores a random anonymous identifier
    in a first-party cookie and browser storage so repeat visits can be counted. Pangaea also loads
    Vercel Web Analytics, which is cookieless. Vercel receives the page URL, referrer, and coarse
    device information for a page view. It does not set a Pangaea cookie. See
    <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy">PostHog's privacy policy</a> and
    <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy">Vercel's privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Essay pages can report a view to Pangaea's own counter. The server stores an aggregate count
    per essay only when a Redis store is configured. It does not store the raw IP address. It keeps
    a short-lived hash so the same visitor is counted once per day, then the hash expires. When the
    store is not configured, the server saves nothing and the directory does not show counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Fonts, media, and embeds</h2>
  <p>
    Pages load Fraunces and Newsreader from Google Fonts, so a browser connects to
    fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com and sends the technical request information needed
    to return those files. Some essays contain a lazy-loaded Spotify player. YouTube and SoundCloud
    players can also appear. Those services receive a request only when a page containing their
    player loads, and their own terms and privacy policies apply. YouTube players use the
    youtube-nocookie.com embed domain.
  </p>

  <h2>Authoring tools and local storage</h2>
  <p>
    The Daily writing page stores drafts and preferences in browser localStorage on the device.
    Daily text stays on that device unless the author enables sync, requests the server-side polish
    tool, or sends text to the Write editor. When sync is enabled, the selected writing data is sent
    to Pangaea's server and stored in a private GitHub repository branch. Publishing through Write
    sends the chosen post content to GitHub so it can become part of the site.
  </p>

  <h2>Authentication and cookies</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub sign-in for author-only tools uses a short-lived state cookie and, after an allowed
    account signs in, an HMAC-signed session cookie. The session cookie is Secure on HTTPS,
    HttpOnly, and SameSite=Lax. Password-gated preview and studio pages can set their own Secure,
    HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict access cookie. These cookies support author access and are not used
    for advertising. GitHub receives information when its sign-in flow is used.
  </p>

  <h2>Feeds and external links</h2>
  <p>
    The RSS feed is a public document and does not require an account. Links can lead to Adam's
    site, published projects, source material, or media services. Following an external link sends
    the destination the normal request information produced by a browser. Pangaea does not control
    how those independent sites handle data.
  </p>
</Base>
